    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p41-q53-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{1}{n(n-1)}=\\dfrac{1}{n-1}-\\dfrac1n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac1{n-1}-\\dfrac1n=\\dfrac1{n(n-1)}$.\n\nCombine the right-hand side over a common denominator.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{n-1}-\\frac1n&=\\frac{n-(n-1)}{n(n-1)}\\\\\\frac1{n-1}-\\frac1n&=\\frac1{n(n-1)}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p41-q53-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the exact value of\n$$\\frac1{\\binom22}+\\frac1{\\binom32}+\\frac1{\\binom42}+\\cdots.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2$.\n\nUse part (a) to telescope the partial sum: $S_N=2(1-\\frac{1}{N})$, which tends to $2$.",
          "worked": "For $n\\geq2$, factorial expansion and part (a) give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{\\binom n2}&=\\frac{2!(n-2)!}{n!}\\\\\\frac1{\\binom n2}&=\\frac2{n(n-1)}\\\\\\frac1{\\binom n2}&=2\\left(\\frac1{n-1}-\\frac1n\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nLet $S_N$ be the sum through $\\frac{1}{\\binom N2}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_N&=2\\left[\\left(1-\\frac12\\right)+\\left(\\frac12-\\frac13\\right)+\\cdots+\\left(\\frac1{N-1}-\\frac1N\\right)\\right]\\\\S_N&=2\\left(1-\\frac1N\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nAs $N$ increases without bound, $\\frac{1}{N}$ tends to $0$. Therefore the exact value of the infinite sum is $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
